  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a comprehensive overview of what opioids are derived from:\n\n---\n\n## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation.\n\nIn its raw state, opium is a gummy latex extracted from *Papaver somniferum*. Cultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun about 3,000 BCE, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and for producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks.\n\n---\n\n### There are three main categories of opioids:\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids\nNatural opioids are substances derived directly from the opium poppy, typically from the resin of the plant's seed pod. Some opioid drugs are made from naturally occurring plant compounds (alkaloids) that come from the opium poppy. Natural opioids such as **codeine**, **heroin**, **morphine**, and **opium** can be extracted from the opium poppy.\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically processing natural opioids, modifying them into new opioid compounds. While these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that pharmaceutical manufacturers (or drug traffickers) use to make semi-synthetic opioids. Hydrocodone (e.g., Vicodin), hydromorphone (e.g., Dilaudid), and oxycodone (e.g., OxyContin, Percocet) may be partially synthesized from chemical components of opium.\n\n### 3. \u2697\ufe0f Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are entirely man-made. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids that are derived from compounds extracted from the opium poppy, synthetic opioids are made from other precursor chemicals that do not depend on the opium poppy. Synthetic opioid compounds are made to mimic natural opioids (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors. Common examples include **fentanyl**, **methadone**, and **tramadol**.\n\n---\n\n### How Do They Work?\nOpioids are a class of drugs that derive from, or mimic, natural substances found in the opium poppy plant, and they work in the brain to produce a variety of effects, including pain relief.\n\n### Risks\nOpioid drugs, even those prescribed by a doctor, are very addictive and can lead to opioid use disorder. Using any opioids for their euphoric effect, even for a short time, raises the risk of substance use disorder, overdose, and death.",
